 "capsule": "In this atlas, 'Georgia' denotes the continuous Georgian national entity known to its own people as Sakartvelo, tracing from the early Christian principality of Kartli (Iberia) under local eristavis and the presiding prince Guaram I (curopalates from c. 575-588), through centuries of Byzantine, Sasanian, Arab and Mongol overlordship, medieval unification and later fragmentation into rival kingdoms, absorption into the Russian Empire after 1801 and the Soviet Georgian SSR after 1921, to the independent republic proclaimed in 1991. Its heartland has always centered on the Kartli-Kakheti plain and South Caucasus highlands between the Black and Caspian Seas, with Tbilisi as its capital since the sixth century. Modern Georgia, independent since 1991, remains organized around this same historic core.",
